---
import { EstateSettings, TAX_SIMPLIFICATIONS, US_FIXED, usRmdStartAge } from '@glideworth/core';
import ToolPage from '../../components/tools/ToolPage.astro';
import { fillTo, ladder, readRoth } from '../../tools/engine/roth-conversion';
import { bracketRates, federalYear, ratePct } from '../../tools/engine/us-tax';
import { money, pct } from '../../tools/format';
import { SPECS } from '../../tools/specs';
import { defaults } from '../../tools/state';
import { externalLinkProps } from '../../lib/links';

const m = (x: number) => money(x, 'USD');
// The worked example is the calculator's defaults, computed by its own code at build time.
const ex = defaults(SPECS['roth-conversion']);
const i = readRoth(ex);
const d = federalYear(i.year);
const fill = fillTo(i);
const lad = ladder(i);
const gain = lad.convert.endAfterTax - lad.none.endAfterTax;
const rows = d.brackets[i.status];
const targetIdx = rows.findIndex(([, r]) => Math.abs(r - i.target) < 1e-12);
const targetTop = rows[targetIdx + 1]?.[0] ?? 0;
const deduction = d.standardDeduction[i.status];
const heirDefault = EstateSettings.parse({}).heirTaxDeferredRate;

// The same couple at 67, with Social Security already paid: the bracket arithmetic stops working.
const older = readRoth({ ...ex, age: 67, age2: 67 });
const olderFill = fillTo(older);
const naive = targetTop + deduction + 2 * d.additionalAged.married;

const byBracket = bracketRates(i.year, i.status)
  .slice(0, -1)
  .map((rate) => {
    const f = fillTo(i, rate);
    const o = fillTo(older, rate);
    return [ratePct(rate), m(f.room), m(f.tax), m(o.room), m(o.tax)];
  });

const sources = d.sources.filter((s) => !/HTML of/.test(s.note ?? ''));
const simplifications = TAX_SIMPLIFICATIONS['us-federal'] ?? [];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How much should I convert to Roth each year?',
    a: '<p>A common approach is to convert up to the top of a bracket you expect to be at or above later, when required distributions, Social Security and a surviving spouse’s single brackets can push income up. This calculator shows the amount for the bracket you choose and the tax on it, and the multi-year comparison shows how a ladder of such conversions plays out against not converting, under the assumptions you enter.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a Roth conversion ladder?',
    a: '<p>Converting part of a traditional IRA or 401(k) to a Roth IRA every year, usually in low-income years between retiring and claiming Social Security or starting required distributions. Each conversion is taxed as ordinary income in the year it is made.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is the conversion smaller once Social Security has started?',
    a: '<p>Because converting also makes more of your Social Security taxable, so each dollar converted adds more than a dollar of taxable income. The calculator finds the amount by running the tax engine again for each step, so this is included.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Who pays the tax on the conversion?',
    a: '<p>In the multi-year comparison the engine pays it from taxable savings first, like your spending. Paying it from outside the IRA is what usually makes a conversion worthwhile; once taxable savings run out, the engine draws on the other accounts.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does this include state tax or Medicare premiums?',
    a: '<p>No. It is a federal income tax estimate. A large conversion can also raise Medicare premiums two years later (IRMAA) and reduce an ACA premium tax credit; the full planner models both, year by year.</p>',
  },
];
---

<ToolPage
  id="roth-conversion"
  heading="Roth conversion calculator"
  lede="How much to convert from a traditional IRA or 401(k) to fill a federal bracket this year, the tax on it, and a multi-year conversion ladder compared with not converting. It runs the Glideworth planning engine in your browser; nothing you type is sent anywhere."
  faq={faq}
  adds="This calculator converts up to one bracket for a fixed number of years, with one steady return and federal tax only."
  related={[
    { href: '/features/tax-estimates/', label: 'Tax estimates in the planner' },
    { href: '/features/solvers/', label: 'Solvers, including the tax strategy search' },
    { href: '/planning/us/', label: 'Retirement planning in the United States' },
  ]}
>
  <h2>How the calculator works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>This year.</strong> The engine’s US federal tax model computes your tax on this year’s income, then adds
      conversion income until the next dollar would fall in a higher ordinary bracket. That is the same search the
      planner’s tax strategy uses. The tax on the conversion is the federal tax with it minus the tax without it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The ladder.</strong> The engine projects your plan twice, year by year, with federal tax estimated every
      year: once converting up to the same bracket for the number of years you choose, once without. Spending and the
      tax on conversions come from taxable savings first; required minimum distributions are taken from the age the
      engine’s rules give for your birth year.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The comparison</strong> counts what is left at the end: Roth and taxable savings in full, traditional
      balances after the tax rate you set for whoever withdraws them ({pct(heirDefault, 0)} by default, the planner’s
      default heir rate).
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>
    Computed by the calculator’s own code when this page was built. A married couple filing jointly in {i.year}, both
    aged {i.age}, with {m(i.trad)} in traditional accounts, {m(i.taxable)} in taxable savings, spending of
    {' '}{m(i.spend)} a year and Social Security of {m(i.ss)} a year from {i.ssAge}. They have no other taxable income
    this year.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The {ratePct(i.target)} bracket ends at {m(targetTop)} of taxable income, and their standard deduction is
      {' '}{m(deduction)}, so they can convert {m(fill.amount)}. The federal tax on it is {m(fill.tax)},
      {' '}{pct(fill.average)} of the amount.
    </li>
    <li>
      Converting like this for {i.years} years moves {m(lad.convert.totalConverted)} into the Roth IRA. Federal tax over
      the plan is {m(lad.convert.lifetimeTax)} with the ladder and {m(lad.none.lifetimeTax)} without. Without conversions,
      required distributions from age {usRmdStartAge(i.year - i.age)} total {m(lad.none.totalRmd)}; with them,
      {' '}{m(lad.convert.totalRmd)}.
    </li>
    <li>
      At {i.planTo}, with traditional balances counted after {pct(i.heir, 0)} tax, their savings are
      {' '}{m(lad.convert.endAfterTax)} with the ladder and {m(lad.none.endAfterTax)} without:
      {' '}{gain >= 0 ? `${m(gain)} more` : `${m(-gain)} less`} with the conversions, in today’s money.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Now the same couple at 67, with Social Security already paid. Bracket arithmetic says they could convert up to
    {' '}{m(naive)} (the top of the bracket plus their standard deduction and the additions for age) less their taxable
    income. But each dollar converted also makes more of their benefits taxable, so the engine finds room for
    {' '}{m(olderFill.amount)}, taxed at {pct(olderFill.average)} on average.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Conversion by bracket">
    <table>
      <caption>Converting up to the top of each bracket in {i.year} (married filing jointly)</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Up to the top of</th><th>At {i.age}: conversion</th><th>Tax on it</th><th>At 67 with Social Security: conversion</th><th>Tax on it</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>{byBracket.map((row) => <tr>{row.map((c) => <td>{c}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Where the figures come from</h2>
  <p>Brackets, deductions and capital gains bands come from the engine’s US tax data, which records its sources:</p>
  <ul>
    {sources.map((s) => <li><a href={s.url} {...externalLinkProps(s.url)}>{s.note ?? s.url}</a></li>)}
    <li>
      <a href={US_FIXED.socialSecurity.source} {...externalLinkProps(US_FIXED.socialSecurity.source)}>IRS Publication 915: Social Security benefits</a>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What this assumes and leaves out</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Federal income tax only: no state tax, no Medicare premium surcharges (IRMAA), no ACA premium tax credit.</li>
    <li>
      The ladder uses one steady return after inflation and no inflation, so every figure is in today’s money and the
      year’s published thresholds keep their real value in later years.
    </li>
    <li>
      Both people live to the end of the plan: the move to single brackets after a death, which often makes
      conversions worth more, is not shown here. The planner’s survivor scenarios model it.
    </li>
    <li>
      The {US_FIXED.rothFiveYearRule}-year rules on Roth withdrawals and the early-withdrawal additional tax are not
      checked. Conversions are taxed as ordinary income in the year made.
    </li>
    <li>An estimate, not tax advice. The engine’s own list of what its US federal model leaves out:</li>
  </ul>
  <details>
    <summary>US federal simplifications ({simplifications.length})</summary>
    <ul>{simplifications.map((s) => <li>{s}</li>)}</ul>
  </details>
</ToolPage>
